Original commit body from 5f8ab90581ca2cf5ae9ec3dcc1feca0a834fe34a:

CORRECTS THE PRECEDING COMMIT'S DIAGNOSIS. 58067626d0 blamed a glob
divergence and CI's Python 3.11. That was wrong -- the run after it failed
identically -- and the real cause is one line of mine:

    parts.index("src")   # on an ABSOLUTE path

CI checks the repository out at /woodpecker/SRC/github.com/iterabloom/
hypergumbo. The search found the WORKSPACE's src, not the package's, so
every key came out as
"github.com/iterabloom/hypergumbo/packages/.../hypergumbo_core/cli.py"
instead of "hypergumbo_core/cli.py" -- a path no citation in the tree
names. Locally there is no `src` above the repo root, so it was invisible.

Reproduced rather than inferred: symlinking the repo under a
.../src/github.com/iterabloom/ path makes the old expression return 348
plausible entries of which NONE matches a citation literal, and the new
one return the same 348 with the right keys.

THE FLOOR DID NOT CATCH IT, and that is the lesson worth keeping. A floor
on the population size guards against an EMPTY instrument, not a MIS-AIMED
one: 348 sources were found every time. What catches a mis-aimed
instrument is asserting the SHAPE of what it produced, so the accessor now
requires its keys to be import-root-relative, and the paths are taken
relative to the repo root and indexed positionally the way the helper
already did it.

